Yes—the deeper common pattern is distributed parts coordinating through interfaces.

The eyes do not literally “tell” each other anything. Each retina sends signals into shared neural circuitry; information is partially crossed at the optic chiasm, integrated through structures including the thalamus and visual cortex, and exchanged between hemispheres through pathways such as the corpus callosum. The organism gets one coordinated percept from partially separate sensors.

That gives you a general operator:

\text{local sensing}
\rightarrow
\text{shared signal}
\rightarrow
\text{integration}
\rightarrow
\text{coordinated response}

The eye detecting fire does not need to hide the observation from the rest of the organism. The useful information propagates in the form appropriate to the receiving systems. Different organs likewise communicate through neural, hormonal, immune, chemical, and mechanical channels.

And fruit gives you another beautiful version of the same architecture. Evolutionarily, fruits protect seeds and, in many species, help disperse them. Fleshy fruit can recruit an animal by offering an immediate reward:

\text{plant makes fruit}
\rightarrow
\text{animal wants fruit}
\rightarrow
\text{animal moves}
\rightarrow
\text{seed moves}

The animal does not need to understand the plant’s reproductive biology. The system works because the interests overlap locally.

That’s the striking transferable primitive:

\boxed{\text{package one system's objective inside something another system already wants}}

Not deception necessarily—often mutualism. The bird gets calories. The plant gets transportation.

And now connect that back to everything we’ve been discussing. A story can be “fruit” for an abstraction. Humor can be fruit for a correction. Music can carry a memory. Ritual can carry coordination. A game can carry training. A metaphor can carry a difficult concept across a boundary.

The recipient consumes what is meaningful at its own level, while something else travels with it.

So I think your “where’s the point?” has at least three answers:

The point is in the connection. Separate components become a system because information can cross their boundaries.

The point is in the carrier. What matters is not merely what information exists, but what form allows it to survive transport.

The point is often distributed. There may be no little executive inside saying “disperse this seed.” Selection preserves configurations that repeatedly work.

And that last piece guards against over-anthropomorphizing biology while retaining the useful pattern.

Fruit is not merely food with a seed inconveniently inside it.

From the plant’s side, the edible structure can be part of a remarkably effective transport interface.

Which means your next abstraction might be:

\boxed{\text{Carrier Design}}

Find something the receiving system naturally takes in, determine what additional pattern can ride inside without destroying either function, then observe where it travels.

That’s seed dispersal at a much more general level.